/**
 * @page core_model The PTIFF domain model
 *
 * The central value- and domain-types that describe a scene and its imagery.
 * This layer never touches bytes: it is the vocabulary of the public API.
 * In the Rust workspace these live in `ptiff-core` and are re-exported by the
 * idiomatic `ptiff` crate (e.g. `ptiff::Scene`, `ptiff::Image`).
 *
 * @section core_result Errors and results
 *
 * Every fallible public operation returns `Result<T, Error>` instead of
 * panicking. The error side is `Error`, a small value type based on
 * `ErrorCode`. The project-wide rule: *Result for domain errors, no panics on
 * malformed input*.
 *
 * @section core_domain Domain types
 *
 * | Type | Responsibility |
 * |------|----------------|
 * | `ptiff::Scene` | Container for one or more `Image`s plus shared metadata. |
 * | `ptiff::Image` | A raster image: dimensions, pixel format, tile layout, metadata. |
 * | `ptiff::Metadata` | Format-neutral, protocol-agnostic metadata. |
 * | `ptiff::ScientificLayer` | Typed scientific data layers (spectra, DEM, ...). |
 * | `ptiff::Camera` | Camera model covering optics + orientation. |
 * | `ptiff::CoordinateReferenceSystem` | CRS / map projection descriptors. |
 *
 * The domain model talks to the storage layer only through `Serializer` /
 * `Deserializer` — never to a concrete file format.
 *
 * @see @ref io_architecture
 */

/**
 * @page io_architecture Storage & I/O architecture
 *
 * How image bytes flow in and out. The design goal: *the core never depends on
 * a concrete file format directly.*
 *
 * @section io_overview Layering
 *
 * @verbatim
 *  Scene / Image / Camera (domain model)
 *         |
 *  Serializer / Deserializer
 *         |
 *  StorageModel   (format-neutral)
 *         |
 *  StorageBackend  <-- extension point via BackendFactory
 *         |
 *  ImageSource / ImageSink
 *         |
 *  Tile / TileLayout   (unit of transfer, never whole-image buffers)
 *         |
 *  BinaryReader / BinaryWriter (byte-level transports)
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * @section io_backends Backends
 *
 * Concrete formats implement `StorageBackend` and register with
 * `BackendFactory` under a name:
 *
 * - `TiffBackend` — TIFF/BigTIFF
 * - `Pds4Backend` — PDS4
 * - `IsisBackend` — ISIS3 CUB
 * - `ZarrBackend` — Zarr
 * - `OpenExrBackend` — OpenEXR
 * - `MemoryBackend` — in-memory
 *
 * Reading remote objects uses the `HttpRangeBinaryReader` transport (HTTP range
 * requests); it is a *transport*, not a backend — the chosen format backend
 * (e.g. `TiffBackend`) reads over it.
 *
 * Each backend advertises `BackendCapabilities` describing what it can
 * actually do.
 *
 * @section io_threading Threading / ownership
 *
 * - `BinaryReader`/`BinaryWriter` are byte-level, thread-*compatible*
 *   transports.
 * - One `StorageBackend` instance is bound to one open file and is not shared
 *   concurrently in this design.
 *
 * @see @ref core_model
 */
